# Psychosis Psychoeducation Newsletter
## Week of November 9, 2026

Recovery conversations need room for ordinary ambitions: remembering an appointment, finding meaningful work, or feeling more comfortable being active. Those ambitions belong to the person experiencing psychosis. Family members can help make space for them without becoming responsible for delivering treatment or measuring someone else's progress.

This week, three influential original trials examine different parts of that picture. One tests support for thinking skills. Another asks whether an employment service delivers the hoped-for benefit in everyday practice. The third tests sustained support for physical health. “Landmark” here means that a paper helped shape an enduring clinical question; it does not mean that its findings are the final answer.

As you read, keep three questions in mind: What changed? Compared with what? Did the change reflect something that mattered to participants? A statistically significant result is evidence against a chance explanation under a study's assumptions. It is not a guarantee of a large benefit, and an inconclusive result is not proof that nobody can benefit.

## 1. Thinking skills: a test score is a starting point

**Citation:** Wykes T, Reeder C, Landau S, Everitt B, Knapp M, Patel A, Romeo R. Cognitive remediation therapy in schizophrenia: randomised controlled trial. *British Journal of Psychiatry*. 2007;190:421–427. [doi:10.1192/bjp.bp.106.026575](https://doi.org/10.1192/bjp.bp.106.026575). PMID: 17470957.

**Study and findings.** This influential trial helped establish cognitive rehabilitation as a testable treatment target. South London community services recruited 85 people with schizophrenia and cognitive and social difficulties: 43 received 40 cognitive-remediation sessions alongside usual care; 42 received usual care. Cognitive remediation means structured practice and strategies for thinking skills. Assessments occurred at baseline, 14 weeks, and 40 weeks.

The estimated working-memory advantage across post-treatment assessments was **1.33 test points (95% confidence interval 0.43–2.16)**; standardized effect size 0.34. Working memory means holding information in mind briefly while using it. The interval describes statistical uncertainty around the estimate. There was no direct treatment effect on social-functioning scores. Memory improvement and social improvement were associated; that does not establish that one caused the other. The small, selected sample, imperfect assessor masking, and absence of an equally intensive attention control limit interpretation. [Original publisher full text](https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/124D7774E0FBECAD43254E3AFDE2E140/S0007125000172150a.pdf/cognitive-remediation-therapy-in-schizophrenia-randomised-controlled-trial.pdf).

**Editorial interpretation for care.** A useful treatment discussion should connect an assessment to an everyday aim. If someone wants to follow a recipe, manage a journey, or return to a course, the question is what support would help with that particular task. A better score alone should not be presented as evidence that all those activities will become easier.

**For families.** Ask permission before offering reminders or organizing tools. “Would a written plan help?” leaves room for an answer; repeatedly testing someone's recall can turn support into an examination. A missed step need not become an argument about effort. The aim is to make a chosen activity more manageable, while preserving the person's control over how help is offered.

Useful questions for the team include: “Would assessment of thinking difficulties help us understand this goal?” “If rehabilitation is offered, how will we judge whether it helps outside the sessions?” Families can also ask which parts of a plan require professional support, rather than assuming that they must reproduce a therapy programme at home.

## 2. Employment: the service behind the referral matters

**Citation:** Howard LM, Heslin M, Leese M, McCrone P, Rice C, Jarrett M, Spokes T, Huxley P, Thornicroft G. Supported employment: randomised controlled trial. *British Journal of Psychiatry*. 2010;196(5):404–411. [doi:10.1192/bjp.bp.108.061465](https://doi.org/10.1192/bjp.bp.108.061465). PMID: 20435968.

**Study and findings.** This important UK implementation trial challenged assumptions about transferring an established service model. In two South London boroughs, 219 unemployed adults with severe mental illness who wanted work were randomized to Individual Placement and Support (IPS; 109) or usual vocational services (110). IPS emphasizes finding ordinary paid work with ongoing support. The sample included psychotic and mood disorders.

At one year, **13/98 followed participants receiving IPS versus 7/99 controls** met the employment outcome: roughly 13% versus 7%, an observed difference of **6.2 percentage points**, calculated from those counts. The outcome required at least 30 days' continuous competitive employment, with part-time work counted pro rata. The difference was not statistically significant (p=0.15). Twenty-two people lacked follow-up; only 73/109 assigned IPS had direct consultant contact. This was an inconclusive local result, not proof that IPS never works. Limited engagement and service integration complicate generalization. [Original full text](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2862060/).

**Editorial interpretation for care.** The enduring lesson is to ask what happens after a referral. A programme name cannot answer whether a person meets a worker, receives help with applications, or can obtain support when difficulties arise. Clinicians can use the paper as a reason to examine delivery and access, without treating a disappointing result as a judgment about people's capacity to work.

**For families.** Begin with the person's own wishes: “Is work something you want to explore now?” A job should not become a test of recovery or a condition for being taken seriously. If work is a goal, offer practical help with one agreed step, and let the person decide whether family involvement is welcome.

Questions for the team might be: “Who will make the first contact?” “What support continues after someone starts work?” “Who can explain how earnings might affect benefits?” These questions turn a general recommendation into a plan with a named person and a next step. If the first referral goes nowhere, asking for a review is reasonable; the family need not become the employment service.

## 3. Physical health: make support accessible and sustained

**Citation:** Daumit GL, Dickerson FB, Wang NY, et al. A behavioral weight-loss intervention in persons with serious mental illness. *New England Journal of Medicine*. 2013;368(17):1594–1602. [doi:10.1056/NEJMoa1214530](https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa1214530). PMID: 23517118.

**Study and findings.** ACHIEVE was a landmark demonstration that a sustained, adapted programme could improve weight outcomes in psychiatric rehabilitation. At 10 Maryland programmes, 291 overweight or obese adults were randomized to tailored individual/group weight-management and exercise sessions (144) or basic information and quarterly unrelated health classes (147). **58.1% had schizophrenia or schizoaffective disorder**; results concern the mixed sample, not a separately proven psychosis subgroup.

Weight change was assessed at six and 18 months. At 18 months, weights were available for 279 participants. The model-estimated between-group difference in weight change was **−3.2 kg (95% confidence interval −5.1 to −1.2)**, favouring intervention. This is an average additional reduction, not everyone's expected loss. The study did not establish fewer heart attacks or longer life. Its rehabilitation setting and exclusions, including active substance-use disorders and inability to walk, limit generalization. [Original full text](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3743095/).

**Editorial interpretation for care.** Physical-health conversations should offer resources rather than assign blame. A plan needs to be feasible for the person who will live with it. Cost, transport, food preferences, physical comfort, and the kind of activity someone enjoys are appropriate subjects for a shared discussion, rather than obstacles to be brushed aside.

**For families.** Ask whether support is wanted before discussing weight. An invitation to share a meal or an enjoyable activity can respect someone's preferences; monitoring food or commenting repeatedly on appearance may not. People deserve respectful physical healthcare whatever happens on the scales.

Useful team questions include: “What accessible programme could support the goals we have agreed?” “Who is coordinating physical-health care?” “How will the plan be reviewed if it is uncomfortable, unaffordable, or difficult to attend?” These are questions about the quality of care, not a request for a family member to supervise another adult's body. This paper does not provide a reason to change medication independently.

## Bringing the papers together

Our editorial reading is that everyday recovery goals deserve specific support and realistic review. A plan can be hopeful while remaining honest about uncertainty. It can also change when the person's priorities change.

For current practice, NICE recommends supported employment for adults with psychosis or schizophrenia who wish to find or return to work, and a combined healthy-eating and physical-activity programme. These recommendations are separate from the three historical trials; availability and referral arrangements depend on the local service. [NICE supported-employment quality statement](https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/qs80/chapter/Quality-statement-5-Supported-employment-programmes), [NICE CG178, physical health](https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg178/chapter/recommendations#physical-health).

For the next appointment, consider choosing just one goal with the person concerned. Agree what help they want, who will provide it, and when to check whether it is useful. “What would make this week easier?” can be a more workable opening than trying to solve every difficulty at once.
